This is an implementation of the quaternary model presented in the PhD thesis of
Robert Berman 1983.

https://open.library.ubc.ca/soa/cIRcle/collections/ubctheses/831/items/1.0052362

with one small typo fix of the S A M M value of the Margules coefficents. In
Beckett's code (and Yoneda's), the correct numbers are printed. Error found and
corrected by Denton Ebel in his implementation of the Berman 1983 model, see
https://research.amnh.org/~debel/vapors1/codes/B83a/B83acts1.txt

The Berman 1983 model describes the system SiO2, MgO, CaO, Al2O3. 

A second correction: the formula of rankinite is printed as Ca2Si3O8 in the
thesis table, but rankinite is Ca3Si2O7, and the thermodynamic data in that
row belong to Ca3Si2O7. Read as Ca3Si2O7, the enthalpy of formation from the
oxides (Berman 1983's own lime and a-quartz) is -44.0 kJ per mole of oxide,
between wollastonite (-43.6) and b-larnite (-44.4); read as Ca2Si3O8 it would
be +11 kJ per mole of oxide, i.e. never stable. This is also consistent with
Berman & Brown 1984 (same S and Cp, formula Ca3Si2O7) and with Holland &
Powell's rankinite (Ca3Si2O7, H = -3944.4 kJ, S = 210 J/K). Only the formula
was changed, in all copies of the thesis table:
  Berman1983/minerals.fwf, minerals_reduced.fwf, minerals_reduced_1.fwf,
  Berman1988/minerals_berman_phd_1983.fwf,
  GhiorsoSack1995/MELTS_Code/minerals_bermanphd.fwf

